The faster accelerator is about 17% more expensive per useful output. Buying System B because 300 is larger than 180 would be naive rationalism: the simple model sounds correct but omits deployment reality.

5. Add energy without double-counting it

Assume average full-system power while provisioned is 0.7 kilowatts for A and 0.9 kilowatts for B. These are illustrative wall-power assumptions, not chip thermal-design ratings.

Producing one million outputs takes about 2.20 paid hours on A and 1.85 hours on B. Energy use is therefore about 1.54 kilowatt-hours for A and 1.67 kilowatt-hours for B.

System B again loses slightly. A higher-power chip can still save energy if it finishes enough useful work faster, so divide power by useful output rather than comparing wattage alone. MLCommons likewise ties full-system wall-power measurements to a specific benchmark result.

6. Software can change the answer without changing the chip

Now suppose better compiler coverage, fused kernels and scheduling raise System B's productive fraction from 50% to 70%. Its effective output becomes 300 × 3,600 × 70% = 756,000 per hour.

Cost falls to about $6.61 per million outputs, and energy falls to about 1.19 kilowatt-hours per million. System B now beats A on both measures.

The hardware did not change. The software and deployment system unlocked more of what was already there.

Quick check — answer before reading on

What productive fraction must System B exceed to match System A's $7.94 cost per million useful outputs?

Answer: at $5.00 per hour, B must produce about 629,700 useful outputs each hour. Dividing that by its maximum 300 × 3,600 outputs gives roughly 58%.

A tempting wrong answer is 42%, found by comparing only the systems' headline speed. The correct break-even point must include both the hourly-price difference and the productive fraction.

Remember these three things

- Compare systems at the same model quality and latency target; otherwise the outputs are not economically equivalent.

- Cost per useful output combines measured workload speed, hourly cost and productive utilization.

- Better software, batching and scheduling can reverse a hardware decision by changing how much paid capacity becomes useful work.

Sources

MLCommons, MLPerf Inference: Datacenter — scenarios, performance, quality targets and full-system power: https://mlcommons.org/benchmarks/inference-datacenter/

NVIDIA, GPU Performance Background User's Guide — peak throughput and workload limits: https://docs.nvidia.com/deeplearning/performance/dl-performance-gpu-background/index.html

NVIDIA, System Management Interface documentation — utilization, memory, clocks and power monitoring: https://docs.nvidia.com/deploy/nvidia-smi/index.html

Google Cloud, Compute pricing — running resources are billed for uptime, including while idle: https://cloud.google.com/products/compute/pricing

All prices, throughput, utilization and power figures in the case are illustrative teaching assumptions, not vendor benchmarks or purchase quotes.

